## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to unify heterogeneous log formats, handle compressed data, and perform time-based validation in a tight, production-like scenario.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-format timestamp extraction (text and JSON)
- Decompression with zcat for .gz files
- Epoch conversion via date -d
- Sliding window filtering (60 minutes) and max absolute skew

## Code Example
```bash
find /logs -type f \( -name '*.log' -o -name '*.log.gz' \) -print0 | while IFS= read -d '' f; do
  case "$f" in
    *.gz) data=$(zcat "$f" 2>/dev/null || echo) ;;
    *) data=$(cat "$f" 2>/dev/null || echo) ;;
  esac
  echo "$data" | awk '
    /^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2} [0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}/ {
      cmd = "date -d \"" $1 " " $2 "\" +%s 2>/dev/null"
      cmd | getline ts; close(cmd)
      if (ts > 0) print ts
    }
    /{.*"ts".*"lvl".*"msg".*}/ {
      match($0, /"ts":[[:space:]]*[0-9]+/)
      if (RSTART > 0) {
        ts = substr($0, RSTART + 5, RLENGTH - 5)
        print ts
      }
    }
  '
done | awk -v now="$(date +%s)" '
  {
    diff = now - $1
    if (diff <= 3600 && diff >= -3600) {
      abs_diff = (diff < 0) ? -diff : diff
      if (abs_diff > max) max = abs_diff
    }
  }
  END { print max }
'
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to handle mixed text and JSON logs, manage log rotation and compression, and perform cross-host aggregation with time-based filtering. It tests regex parsing, time-windowing, deduplication logic, and working within POSIX shell constraints without external dependencies.

## Key Concepts
- POSIX shell constraints with gzipped logs and mixed formats
- Time-window filtering for the last 30 minutes
- Robust user extraction from both plaintext and JSON logs
- Case-insensitive deduplication with per-host aggregation

## Code Example
```bash
for host in $(awk -F'[/=]' '/^\/(logs|var)\/log\// && !/^#/ {print $3}' /etc/hosts 2>/dev/null | sort -u); do
  users=$(find "/logs" -name '*.log*' -mmin -30 2>/dev/null | \
    xargs -I{} sh -c 'zcat -f "{}" 2>/dev/null' | \
    awk -v host="$host" '
      BEGIN {now=systime(); cutoff=now-1800}
      /^[[:space:]]/ {next}
      /^"?[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2} [0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2}/ {
        gsub(/[\[\]:]/, "", $1" "$2)
        cmd="date -d \""$1" "$2"\" +%s 2>/dev/null || date -d 0 +%s"
        cmd | getline ts; close(cmd)
        if (ts >= cutoff) {
          if ($0 ~ /ERROR|WARN/) {
            for (i=1; i<=NF; i++) {
              if ($i ~ /^user[=:]/) {
                gsub(/[,:"\047]/, "", $(i+1))
                print tolower($(i+1))
              }
            }
          }
        }
        next
      }
      /^"?\{/ {
        if (match($0, /"ts"[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*"([^"]+)"/, t)) {
          cmd="date -d \""t[1]"\" +%s 2>/dev/null || date -d 0 +%s"
          cmd | getline ts; close(cmd)
          if (ts >= cutoff && match($0, /"user"[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*"([^"]+)"/, u)) {
            print tolower(u[1])
          }
        }
        next
      }
    ' | sort -u | wc -l)
  echo "$host: $users unique users"
done
```
